WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.880
So, without further ado,

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.640
may I present the founder and
director of NutritionFacts.org,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:16.080
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:22.160
There may be no such thing
as dying from old age.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.880
From a study of more than
42,000 consecutive autopsies,

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.960
centenarians—
those who live to be a hundred—

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.840
were found to have
succumbed-to-disease

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.320
in a hundred percent
of the cases.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.000
Though most were perceived,
even by their physicians,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.920
to have been healthy
just prior to death,

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.000
not one,
“died, of old age.”

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.800
They died from disease,
most commonly, heart disease.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.280
If aging kills via diseases,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.080
why wasn’t my book
How Not to Die

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.400
all the longevity book
anyone needs?

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.760
In it, I ran through
preventing, arresting,

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.120
or reversing each
of our top 15 killers—

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.440
starting with
heart disease,

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.680
not only the #1 killer
of centenarians,

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.840
but of men and women
across the board

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:16.480
and projected to remain that
way in the decades to come.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.640
But is it really?

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.760
Because the single
greatest risk factor

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.800
for most of our
killer diseases

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.920
is how old you are,

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.040
one could argue that the
leading cause of death

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.480
is actually aging.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.080
The rate of death
increases exponentially

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.760
for age-related
diseases,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.880
such as heart disease,
cancer, stroke, dementia.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.480
So yes, within the
same age bracket,

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.200
having a high cholesterol

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.080
can increase your risk of
heart disease up to 20-fold,

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:58.320
but an 80-year-old may have
five hundred times the risk

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.200
of having a heart attack

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.920
compared to a
twenty-year-old.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.280
Now, the reason we focus
on things like cholesterol

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.640
is because it’s a
modifiable risk factor,

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:17.120
but what if the rate of
aging was modifiable too?

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.840
Instead of our current
piecemeal approach

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.440
of focusing on
individual diseases,

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.840
what about slowing down
the aging process itself?

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.880
When I was a
nerdy little kid,

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:35.080
I wanted to cure
cancer when I grew up.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.040
But even if all forms
of cancer were eliminated,

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.960
the average life
expectancy in the U.S.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.360
would only go up
about three years.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:45.960
Why?

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.640
Because dodging cancer
would just mean delaying death

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.280
from a heart attack or stroke.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.280
If one age-related ailment
doesn’t get us, another will.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.200
So, rather than playing
“whack-a-mole”

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:00.880
by tackling each
disease separately,

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.960
slowing the
rate of aging

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:07.720
could potentially address
all these issues simultaneously.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:11.040
Imagine if there
were an intervention

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.560
that didn’t just reduce your
risk of the leading killers,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.720
but also arthritis,
osteoporosis,

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.320
sensory impairments.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:22.800
Because risks tend to
double every seven years—

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.200
like if the average 65-year-old
had the health of a 58-year-old?

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.760
Slowing aging by
even just seven years

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.200
could cut everyone’s
risk of death, frailty,

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.400
and disability in half.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.680
That’s why I wrote
How Not to Age.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.680
The problem is that
the anti-aging field

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.040
is said to be a “fertile
ground for cons,

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.880
scams, and get-rich-
quick schemes.”

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.320
As a former president of the
Gerontological Society wrote,

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.000
there have been “few subjects
which have been more misleading

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:02.880
to the uncritical and more
proﬁtable to the unscrupulous.”

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.400
Not only does the popular
literature on the subject

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.560
harbor a “huge amount
of misinformation,”

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.560
but most age-researching
scientists widely known to the public

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:18.720
are said to be “unscrupulous
purveyors of useless nostrums,”

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.120
according to the Editor-in-Chief
of a leading gerontology journal.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.240
When it comes to something
as life-and-death important

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.240
as to what to feed
ourselves and our families,

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:34.360
we should rely not on
anecdote, but on evidence.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:37.720
That’s why I cite
everything to the teeth.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:41.000
How Not to Die,
had about 2,000 citations.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.840
How Not to Diet,
5,000 citations.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:53.680
How Not to Age ended
up with 13,000 citations,

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:56.320
all of which I have
hyperlinked for you online

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.560
to access all the
original sources.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.760
So, you can
download the studies

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:11.000
and read them
all yourself.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.760
My aim was to cover
every possible angle

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.280
for developing the
optimal diet and lifestyle,

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.200
for the longest,
healthiest lifespan—

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:22.280
based on the best available
balance of evidence.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:26.440
My inspiration for writing the book
was a consensus document,

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.240
“Interventions to Slow
Aging in Humans”,

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.560
compiled by the top researchers
in the anti-aging field

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.480
(the likes of Drs. Longo,
Sinclair, Fontana).

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.400
They were
brought together

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.200
to identify the most
promising strategies

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:44.280
to combat aging.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.960
They identified a list
of “essential pathways,”

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:53.360
for example, drugs that
can block the hormone IGF-1,

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.920
or drugs to block the
pro-aging enzyme TOR.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:59.520
But I realized as
I looked at this:

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.320
Wait, every single
one of these pathways

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.360
could be regulated
through diet.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:11.240
That became the first
section of the book.

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.800
The term “antiaging”

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.760
has been much
abused in popular culture,

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.440
attached to all manner
of unproven products

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:20.120
and procedures,

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.520
whereas the term
should really be reserved

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.680
for things that can
delay or reverse aging

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:26.480
by targeting one
of the established

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:27.680
aging mechanisms,

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:31.040
the so-called
Hallmarks of Aging,

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:34.200
the common denominators
of the aging process like,

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:38.320
for example, the buildup
of misfolded proteins

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:42.640
that can be disposed of
through autophagy.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.440
See, at any given time,

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.120
most of our cells
are producing

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:51.120
and assembling more
than 10,000 distinct proteins,

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.440
each of which can
become misfolded

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:55.240
or damaged
at any time

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:58.200
and require a
cleanup in aisle three.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:02.920
But during times
of nutrient excess,

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:06.880
our body figures
why bother?

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:09.440
We can just toss it in
the corner and make another.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:11.280
Having evolved in
a context of scarcity,

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.840
our body expects to fall
on hard times any day now

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:17.120
and can put off spring
cleaning until then.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.520
But these days,

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:20.360
these lean times
hardly ever come;

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:25.240
so, our cells just end up
continually hoarding junk.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.400
That’s where autophagy
comes from,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.600
from the Greek words
for “self-eating.”

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.120
It’s a housekeeping
process

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:37.960
by which defective cellular
components are broken down

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:39.560
and scrapped
for spare parts.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:43.640
This doubles as both salvage
operation and quality control,

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:46.240
clearing out some
of the damaged debris

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:48.880
implicated in the
aging process,

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:53.080
while renewing our cells
in a sort of cellular reset.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:59.320
As one review put it,
“The janitor is the undercover boss.”

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:03.400
Our ancient ancestors often
went for several days without food;

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.880
so, autophagy was
constantly being switched on.

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:08.000
But these days,

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:10.840
our cells no longer need
to clean out the corners

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:12.160
for sustenance.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.600
And so, the tainted heaps
just pile higher and higher,

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:16.440
which isn’t good,

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:22.040
because autophagy is
essential for lifespan extension.

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:23.760
It’s not only necessary,

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.920
but sometimes sufficient
for increasing longevity.

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:30.840
Self-digestion for
lifespan extension.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.040
Boosting autophagy
alone can boost lifespan,

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:35.120
at least in mice,

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:40.120
by an average of 12 percent,
and it also boosts healthspan.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:44.080
Unfortunately, our bodies’
ability to take-out-the-trash

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.760
declines with age,
leading to this vicious cycle.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.280
Garbage builds up,
accelerating aging,

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:54.200
which leads to more
garbage buildup.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.640
No wonder the drug
industry is so eager

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:00.880
to pharmacologically modulate
autophagy to combat aging—

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.120
but we can do it naturally.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:06.920
One way is
through fasting.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:07.660
Unfortunately,

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:10.640
autophagy doesn’t
really maximally ramp up

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:12.360
until after a day
or two of fasting,

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:15.560
which may be too long
to do it unsupervised.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.000
Fasting more than 24 hours

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:20.200
should probably only be done
under medical supervision,

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:22.320
ideally in a
live-in clinic.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.200
In other words,
don’t try this at home.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.920
This is not just legalistic
mumbo-jumbo.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:28.760
For example,
normally,

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:33.600
your kidneys dive into
sodium conservation mode,

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.680
but should that
response break down,

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:38.760
you could rapidly develop
an electrolyte abnormality

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:41.720
that might only manifest
with non-specific symptoms

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:43.280
like fatigue or dizziness,

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:47.760
which could be easily
dismissed, until it’s too late.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:48.840
Thankfully,

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.200
there’s another way
to activate autophagy.

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.400
You can fast or go fast.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.560
Exercise induces
autophagy.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:58.720
As one of the
researchers said,

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:01.480
“I’ve always known
exercise was good for you,

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:04.440
but when we found that
it increases autophagy,

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:08.320
I finally got a treadmill.”

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:10.000
You have to do
enough, though.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:13.160
Autophagy is activated
after 60 minutes

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.080
of moderate
intensity exercise,

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:20.000
but only twenty minutes
fails to move the needle.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:24.560
What about diet?
Any foods to avoid?

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:27.880
In 2021, we found
out acrylamide

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:29.760
can inhibit autophagy,

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:31.520
at least in cells
in a petri dish.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:33.360
Acrylamide is a toxin

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.200
concentrated in French
fries and potato chips

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.760
that’s formed during
the frying process.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:42.400
The fact that high acrylamide
exposure is associated

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:45.680
with as much as double
the risk of premature death

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:49.480
would be consistent with
an anti-autophagy effect,

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:54.360
though diminished lifespans
among eaters of fatty, salty snacks

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:56.800
isn’t exactly a revelation.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:59.800
You can’t know if
it's cause and effect,

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:04.996
until you… put it to the test—
very nice!

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:09.480
Before being asked to
eat a bag of potato chips

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:11.040
every day for a month,

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:14.560
study subjects were given
weeks of boiled potatoes

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:17.600
mixed with the same amount
of fat and salt as the chips.

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:20.880
Compared with the fatty
salty boiled potatoes,

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:25.600
their C-reactive protein levels
shot up 50 percent on the chips,

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:28.640
suggesting “chronic ingestion
of acrylamide-containing products

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:33.320
induces a proinﬂammatory state.”

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:36.240
However, deep frying
causes the formation

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.760
of all manner of
“undesired food-borne toxicants;”

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:43.400
so, we can’t be sure
it was the acrylamide.

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:46.480
As one of the earliest
geriatric medicine textbooks

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:51.800
presciently concluded
back in 1849,

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:59.040
“frying…is an abomination.”

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.760
If you just must
have your French fry fix,

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:10.120
air-frying produces about
90 percent less acrylamide.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:12.280
Anything that can help?

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:15.520
Starving yourself
“generates discomfort,”

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:18.200
but there is something
that activates autophagy

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:21.760
that many people
find comforting:

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:27.600
coffee.

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:29.200
At a human-
equivalent dose,

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:33.240
both regular and decaf rapidly
induce autophagy within hours—

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:33.960
in mice,

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:37.160
and coffee can extend
the lifespans of some rats,

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:38.400
but what about people?

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.080
In humans, we only
have observational research,

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:42.560
but to date
about 20 studies

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:45.400
have followed more than
10 million people over time,

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:48.120
and those drinking
3 cups (720 ml) of coffee a day

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:50.880
had 13 percent lower risk

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:55.000
of dying from all
causes put together.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.600
And decaf appears
to be just as protective;

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:01.480
so, it’s not the caffeine.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:05.560
Coffee contains more than
a thousand bioactive compounds.

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:07.600
The polyphenol chlorogenic acid

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:10.760
is the most abundant
antioxidant in coffee;

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:13.360
so, researchers started there,
and indeed,

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:18.560
was found to enhance
autophagy in human cells.

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:20.960
More than a hundred
coffees have been tested,

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:25.960
and chlorogenic acid levels
vary by more than 30-fold.

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:31.760
Interestingly, the major contributor
to this wide range is Starbucks,

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:35.680
with its extremely low
chlorogenic acid content,

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:40.640
thought to be because they
roast their beans so dark

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:45.280
that they destroy it.

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:49.080
Freeze-drying is okay,

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:54.040
and brewed has
more than espresso.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:56.400
Paper filtered
is the best,

00:13:56.400 --> 00:13:59.920
because it traps the cholesterol-
raising compounds in coffee,

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:03.440
perhaps explaining why
those drinking filtered coffee

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:09.760
had even lower mortality rates
than those drinking unfiltered coffee.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:13.480
Any food components
that activate autophagy?

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:14.320
Spermidine,

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:19.400
the longevity elixir,
spermidine.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:27.920
Don’t be put off
by the name.

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:31.160
Spermidine and its
byproduct spermine

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:33.520
are actually found
throughout the body.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:37.560
When it was later independently
discovered in the brain and the muscles,

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.160
they called it
neuridine and musculamine

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:42.680
until they found out it was
all the same compound,

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:47.720
and so, naming rights defaulted
to the less palatable original.

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:50.480
Our body can
make it from scratch,

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:54.320
but we can also boost levels
by eating spermidine-rich foods,

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:56.440
which is good news

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:00.120
because our spermidine
levels tend to decline with age,

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:03.560
dropping more than half
by the time we reach our 50s.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:07.200
This decline is seen across
the biological spectrum

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:10.720
with one
remarkable exception,

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:13.400
naked mole rats,

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:15.960
also known by their
more cuddly nickname:

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:23.840
sand puppy,

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:28.680
considered to be a
“non-aging mammal”

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:30.720
without any visible
signs of aging

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:35.480
and almost no decline
in physiological function over decades,

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:37.960
or typical signs of aging,
like loss of fertility or muscle mass.

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:40.600
Perhaps in part

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:46.280
because they are able to maintain
their high levels of spermidine,

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:51.440
something you also see
in human centenarians.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:54.400
To prove cause and effect,
extra spermidine was fed to animals

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:58.840
and “Induction of Autophagy by
Spermidine Promotes Longevity,”

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:03.600
increasing the lifespans of mice,
for example, by as much as 25 percent.

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:08.400
In a database of more than
a thousand life-extending compounds,

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.920
among the small subset
with the fewest side effects,

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:16.360
spermidine has the largest
documented lifespan extension.

00:16:16.360 --> 00:16:19.960
Longevity can be improved
even when started late in life,

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:21.960
kind of the human equivalent
of changing your diet

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:23.640
when you’re already
in your fifties.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.880
Anti-aging effects were
found in the heart and kidneys,

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:30.440
rejuvenating immune function,
delaying brain aging,

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:32.600
and improving
cognitive function.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:36.440
Yeah, but that was in animals
like fruit flies and mice.

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:42.560
I mean who cares if spermidine
cures flies of senior moments.

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:45.880
What about in people?

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:47.760
Hundreds of men and women
in their 40s through 80s

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:48.760
were followed for twenty years,

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:52.400
and after looking at 146
different components of their diets,

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:57.240
the single most predictive
of longevity was spermidine.

00:16:57.240 --> 00:16:58.520
How much spermidine?

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:03.240
Higher spermidine intake
is linked to lower mortality.

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:05.400
Those who consumed
the most spermidine

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:08.120
had a reduced risk of death
from all major causes,

00:17:08.120 --> 00:17:11.240
which is what we’d expect
from an anti-aging compound.

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:13.800
Critically, this survival
advantage persisted

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:16.040
even after controlling
for dietary excellence,

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:17.080
meaning that it
wasn’t just because

00:17:17.080 --> 00:17:18.880
they were eating
healthier foods in general,

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:23.160
but rather spermidine-
rich foods in particular.

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:24.880
How big of an effect
are we talking?

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:27.000
The reduction
in mortality risk

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:29.680
between getting more than
12 mg of spermidine a day

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:31.600
compared to
getting less than 9

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:33.760
was as if those eating
more spermidine

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:36.680
were 5.7 years younger.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:39.200
It’s as if by eating
more of certain foods,

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:44.200
they were effectively able
to turn back the clock nearly 6 years.

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:45.840
The findings were
so extraordinary,

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:48.600
the researchers sought
to replicate their results

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:50.640
in a whole new
set of individuals

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:57.160
and, indeed, arrived at
the same conclusion.

00:17:57.160 --> 00:17:59.600
This led some to propose

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:03.920
that spermidine may
be an anti-aging vitamin.

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:05.120
When we’re younger,

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:07.480
we seem to be able
to make enough,

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:09.880
but as we get older,

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:15.560
we may need to start ensuring
we’re getting enough in our diet

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:18.080
to maintain autophagy
into old age.

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:20.560
Well, if spermidine is going to be
considered an anti-aging vitamin,

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:23.080
where is that
vitamin found?

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:27.000
Beans are said to have
the highest natural amounts,

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:30.600
but I compiled a list of the
top spermidine sources,

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:36.760
and pig pancreas beat out
bean burritos for the bronze.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:38.120
This is virtually every food

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:40.680
averaging at least 2 mg
of spermidine per serving,

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:45.240
with a fermented whole soy food
called tempeh leading the list,

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:47.720
along with plain
white mushrooms.

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:50.560
While some have suggested
the genetic engineering

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:51.920
of spermidine-rich crops,

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:57.280
there are already a plethora
of naturally spermidine-rich foods.

00:18:57.280 --> 00:19:01.960
As a certified dark
green leafy snob,

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:06.480
I was begrudgingly impressed
to see lettuce score so high,

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:07.600
though lettuce is so light,

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:10.120
a 100-gram serving
would be about 3 cups,

00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:15.280
but even the spermidine in
little side salads could really add up.

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:18.080
In the book, I spend a lot
of time going through the list,

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:22.840
but the single most concentrated
source is wheat germ,

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:24.840
with 2.5 mg of spermidine

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:27.760
in just the 7 grams
in a tablespoon.

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:29.720
It’s also the
cheapest source,

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:32.360
costing as little as
two cents per milligram.

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:38.960
You can’t get a lot
of pancreas for two cents.

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:42.560
Does wheat germ
actually do anything?

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:47.000
Let’s randomize people to
some dinner rolls and find out.

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:48.600
A randomized,
double-blind pilot

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:49.480
in which older individuals

00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:51.600
were secretly slipped
some spermidine

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:54.280
in the form of wheat germ
baked into bread rolls

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:57.720
vs placebo rolls with
wheat bran instead.

00:19:57.720 --> 00:20:01.720
And, those with mild dementia
improved way beyond

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:05.760
all available antidementia
drug treatments so far.

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:07.880
Admittedly, that’s
not saying much,

00:20:07.880 --> 00:20:12.440
but what’s the harm of sprinkling
a little wheat germ on your food?

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:18.320
The latest Alzheimer's drugs
don’t appear to work at all.

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:24.440
All you get for
your 56,000 dollars

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:32.360
is a dramatically increased risk
of swelling or bleeding into your brain.

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:35.720
When the FDA
approved it anyway,

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:40.080
the head of the
American Geriatrics Society replied,

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:45.000
“My head just exploded.”

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:49.200
(Maybe they just got
slipped a dose of the drug).

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:51.200
Anyway, lots of other
clinical spermidine studies,

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:52.720
but just to wrap
up this section,

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:57.040
autophagy is considered the
“primary system for cleaning the body”

00:20:57.040 --> 00:20:59.080
from the inside out.

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:02.680
And, we can boost autophagy
with aerobic exercise,

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:06.080
skipping fries and chips,
drinking coffee,

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:07.520
and eating specific foods

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:12.680
to reach a target of
20 mg of spermidine a day.

00:21:12.680 --> 00:21:14.840
I offer similar diet
and lifestyle takeaways

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:18.000
for each of the other
10 anti-aging pathways,

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:19.880
but don’t have time
to touch on them,

00:21:19.880 --> 00:21:25.120
because there’s still three
other major sections of the book.

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:28.520
Part Two focuses on
the diets and lifestyles

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:33.360
of the healthiest and longest-lived
populations around the world.

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:36.920
The odds of living to a hundred have
risen from approximately 1 in 20 million

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:38.600
to as high as 1 in 50.

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:41.440
Why do some make it
and others not?

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:45.880
It’s not just a matter of
picking better parents.

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:51.360
Studies following identical twins
suggest that only about 25 percent

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:55.040
of the variation in lifespan
is explained by genetics.

00:21:55.040 --> 00:21:57.000
So, what can we
do for the majority

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:00.160
over which we may
have some control?

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:03.800
The media loves stories
of hard-living centenarians

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:07.480
who attribute their longevity to
some combination of lard, vodka,

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:10.520
and favorite brand of cigarette,

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:17.200
but how do the oldest and
healthiest really eat and live?

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:20.280
That’s what we have
the blue zones for,

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:25.080
areas of exceptional
longevity around the world,

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:28.360
where there may
be ten times the rate

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:32.040
of those reaching
those triple digits.

00:22:32.040 --> 00:22:36.080
(Named for the color a demographer
used in a global “heat map”

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:38.080
of mortality around the world.)

00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:41.960
What lessons can we learn?

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:44.320
The Blue Zones organization
distilled findings

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:46.440
from more than
150 dietary surveys

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:48.080
from the world’s
longest-living people

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:51.320
to create a set of
ten food guidelines.

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:54.280
The foundation of the
Blue Zones Food Guidelines,

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:58.360
is making your diet at least
95 percent plant-based,

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:00.360
avoiding highly
processed foods,

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:03.160
emphasizing beans as the
healthiest source of protein,

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:07.240
water as the best beverage,
and nuts as the healthiest snack.

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:09.640
That’s the foundation.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:12.080
The final five guidelines
are to “Go easy on fish,”

00:23:12.080 --> 00:23:14.000
“Eliminate eggs,
“Slash sugar,”

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:17.280
“Reduce dairy,”
and “Retreat from meat,”

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:18.920
noting that blue zones
centenarians

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:20.480
only eat about
two ounces (57 g) of meat

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:23.640
or less about five
times a month.

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:27.960
Historically, there’ve been
five recognized blue zones,

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:32.440
but only one survives
and thrives to this day,

00:23:32.440 --> 00:23:35.040
the red, white, and blue zone:

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:38.880
the Seventh-Day Adventists
in Loma Linda, California,

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:41.200
with perhaps the
longest life expectancy

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:46.280
of any formally studied
population in history.

00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:49.880
There are a number of shared
Blue Zone lifestyle characteristics:

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:51.800
family coherence,
avoiding smoking,

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:53.960
daily exercise,
social engagement,

00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:59.760
but plant-based nutrition appears
to be the principal component,

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:03.480
alone accounting for about
half the difference in lifespan.

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:07.400
No surprise, since the number
one risk factor for death

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:12.600
in the United States
is the American diet.

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:20.640
So, unsafe sex is bad.
Sedentary lifestyle? Bad.

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:25.280
Alcohol and drugs—
especially tobacco. Bad.

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:28.840
But cigarettes only kill about
a half million Americans every year,

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:31.360
whereas our diet
kills many more.

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:33.600
We are what we eat.

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:36.200
Which is good news,

00:24:36.200 --> 00:24:40.280
because it means
we have the power.

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:43.800
Changing from a more typical
diet to a more optimized diet

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:45.640
starting at age twenty
would be expected

00:24:45.640 --> 00:24:47.000
to increase the
lifespan of women

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:50.720
by about eleven years
and men by thirteen years.

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:53.880
The largest life
expectancy gain

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:57.259
would be made by
eating more legumes—

00:24:57.259 --> 00:25:00.160
in other words, more beans,
split peas, chickpeas, and lentils.

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:03.120
So, if there was one
thing we could eat:

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:11.680
Legumes for longevity.
Hummus for health!

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:13.960
Then, comes whole
grains and nuts,

00:25:13.960 --> 00:25:17.640
and eating less meat.

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:21.840
Now, for the few of you
who aren’t twenty anymore,

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:23.680
not to worry.

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:26.200
Starting to eat healthier
at age sixty,

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:30.560
could still mean adding eight or
nine more years to your lifespan.

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:34.720
Even starting as late as
age eighty could add years.

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:37.360
Changing your
health destiny

00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:41.560
can start tomorrow
morning at breakfast.

00:25:41.560 --> 00:25:43.600
And it doesn’t take much.

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:46.120
The NIH-AARP study
is the largest

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:48.760
forward-looking study
on diet and health in history.

00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:54.240
Based on its six million
person-years of observation,

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:57.680
replacing just three percent
of daily caloric intake

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:02.160
from animal protein
with plant protein

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:05.240
was associated with a
ten percent decreased risk

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:07.120
of overall mortality
in both men and women.

00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:08.520
Just swapping three percent!

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:14.600
Of all the animal protein sources,
eggs were found to be the worst.

00:26:14.600 --> 00:26:17.560
Swapping in three percent
of plant protein for egg protein

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:19.560
was associated with
twice the benefit,

00:26:19.560 --> 00:26:24.880
twenty percent lower mortality
for swapping in a few British beans

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:27.880
for breakfast instead.

00:26:27.880 --> 00:26:31.520
Harvard researchers found that
when it comes to premature death,

00:26:31.520 --> 00:26:34.200
processed meat was the worst,
followed by eggs.

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:36.120
In essence, they found
that tuna salad

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:38.040
may be better than
egg salad or a BLT,

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:40.840
but a bean burrito
beat out the bunch.

00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:44.240
When it came to death
from all causes put together,

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:47.120
plant protein beat out
every type of animal protein—

00:26:47.120 --> 00:26:49.920
including dairy,
fish, and chicken.

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:52.720
A three percent swap
from chicken to chickpeas,

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:54.320
or fish to falafel

00:26:54.320 --> 00:27:00.600
was linked to a six percent
decreased risk of premature death.

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:06.480
But does eating healthy
actually slow down aging?

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:08.000
Randomize
hundreds of women

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:11.880
to a diet centered around
healthy plant foods,

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:14.920
or exercise, or neither,

00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:19.160
and though the physical
activity failed,

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:21.840
those in the plant-based
dietary intervention group

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:26.800
had a significant slowing
of biological aging.

00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:29.720
Of course, it isn’t just about
adding years to your life,

00:27:29.720 --> 00:27:31.440
but life to your years.

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:34.840
An unhealthy aging
index was devised

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:36.360
to measure functional
impairments,

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:40.440
vitality, mental health,
physical health.

00:27:40.440 --> 00:27:45.640
Substituting even just
one percent of calories

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:48.080
from plant protein
for animal protein

00:27:48.080 --> 00:27:51.400
appeared to lead to significantly
less deficit accumulation.

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:55.080
And substituting five percent
may reduce the risk of dying

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:59.440
from the greatest deficit,
dementia.

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:03.520
That may help explain why those
who don’t eat any meat at all

00:28:03.520 --> 00:28:07.640
may be up to three times
less likely to become demented.

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:09.480
But again,
it’s not all-or-nothing.

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:11.880
In fact, the worst thing
about humanity’s diet

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:16.800
is neither animal nor
vegetable, but mineral:

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:17.920
sodium.

00:28:17.920 --> 00:28:21.160
Here are the top five
fatal flaws of our diet.

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:23.920
Millions of deaths may
be attributable every year

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:25.760
to not getting enough
whole grains,

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:28.640
not eating enough fruit,
not enough nuts and seeds,

00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:29.520
not enough vegetables.

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:33.520
But the single deadliest
ingredient in humanity’s diet

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:35.920
is something we
get too much of:

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:36.800
and that’s salt,

00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:40.320
our number one dietary
risk factor for death.

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:41.980
A recent whopping study,
for example,

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:43.440
of more than a
half million people

00:28:43.440 --> 00:28:48.280
found that those who salted
their food at the table at age fifty

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:52.400
appeared to have about a
two-year lower life expectancy

00:28:52.400 --> 00:28:55.600
compared to those
that didn’t.

00:28:55.600 --> 00:28:57.800
So, just swapping
out the salt shaker

00:28:57.800 --> 00:28:59.080
for some salt-
free seasoning

00:28:59.080 --> 00:29:02.360
or salt substitutes could
potentially add years to your life.

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:05.000
How do we know
it’s cause and effect?

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:08.200
Five kitchens at a
veterans’ retirement home

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:12.640
were randomized into
two groups for a few years,

00:29:12.640 --> 00:29:16.000
offering meals salted either
with regular salt or,

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:17.640
unbeknownst to them,

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:20.880
a 50/50 blend of regular salt,
sodium chloride,

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:25.320
with a salt substitute like these,
potassium chloride.

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:28.080
The kind of salt was the only
difference between the meals,

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:33.280
and cardiovascular disease death
rates plummeted by 40 percent

00:29:33.280 --> 00:29:36.720
in the folks getting the
reduced sodium blend.

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:38.560
The new difference
in life expectancy

00:29:38.560 --> 00:29:40.120
between the two
groups at age seventy

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:41.960
was equivalent
to that which

00:29:41.960 --> 00:29:47.600
would have occurred
naturally in 14 years,

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:50.860
meaning simply switching
to even half potassium salt—

00:29:50.860 --> 00:29:54.680
for which you wouldn’t even
be able to taste the difference of—

00:29:54.680 --> 00:29:57.680
appeared to effectively make
people more than a decade younger

00:29:57.680 --> 00:30:01.880
when it came to the
risk of premature death.

00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:04.680
What do the likes
of the Salt Institute

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:07.400
have to say about public
health recommendations

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:09.440
to reduce sodium intake?

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:12.040
In testimony before a
Congressional committee,

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:14.160
the presumption
that healthier diets

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:17.640
would cut healthcare
costs was challenged.

00:30:17.640 --> 00:30:22.080
“Indeed,” one processed
food defender testified,

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:27.000
“healthcare expenditures increase
if the lifespan is prolonged.”

00:30:28.680 --> 00:30:30.880
If people eat healthier
and live longer,

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:34.120
it could be
more expensive,

00:30:34.120 --> 00:30:36.360
noting that if tobacco
were banned,

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:39.000
the increase in
expected lifespan would,

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:47.120
“increase the cost of
care of old people.”

00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:49.160
Think how expensive
it would be

00:30:49.160 --> 00:30:51.000
if people started taking
care of themselves

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:57.200
and didn’t, conveniently,
die on time.

00:30:57.200 --> 00:30:59.160
Ultra-processed foods,

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:01.800
often packed with added
salt, sugar, and fat,

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:09.480
consistently account for more than
50 percent of our dietary caloric intake.

00:31:09.480 --> 00:31:14.400
More than half of
our diet is junk.

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:15.920
Not surprisingly,

00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:21.440
those foods are associated with
a greater risk of dying prematurely.

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:26.760
So, just cutting back on
animal foods isn’t enough.

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:28.720
Healthy plant-based
diets are associated

00:31:28.720 --> 00:31:30.560
with a significantly
lower risk of dying,

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:34.480
but unhealthy plant-
based diets are not.

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:36.040
In the Harvard cohorts,

00:31:36.040 --> 00:31:38.760
the more you minimize
meat, eggs, and dairy,

00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:41.560
the lower your
risk of death falls,

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:45.440
but that’s only if you’re
eating healthy plant foods.

00:31:45.440 --> 00:31:47.440
If you, instead,
just pile on the junk,

00:31:47.440 --> 00:31:48.440
like chips and soda,

00:31:48.440 --> 00:31:51.880
you can increase your
risk of death overall—

00:31:51.880 --> 00:31:56.360
even if animal product
consumption remains low.

00:31:56.360 --> 00:31:59.520
Same in the Million
Veteran Program study:

00:31:59.520 --> 00:32:03.160
Healthy plant foods,
reduced their risk of death.

00:32:03.160 --> 00:32:09.560
But if your idea of a plant-
based diet is fries and a Coke,

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:12.880
you’re not doing your
body any favors.

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:14.920
Same with the risk
of cognitive impairment.

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:19.920
Only healthy plant
foods reduce risk.

00:32:19.920 --> 00:32:22.800
The same with dementia
and depression.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:24.640
The same with frailty.

00:32:24.640 --> 00:32:30.680
Healthy plant foods good;
plant-based junk bad.

00:32:30.680 --> 00:32:34.720
That’s why Cornell Professor
Emeritus of Biochemistry,

00:32:34.720 --> 00:32:35.520
T. Colin Campbell,

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:39.400
coined the term
whole food, plant-based diet.

00:32:39.400 --> 00:32:40.920
As a physician,

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:43.880
terms like vegetarian or vegan
just tell me what you don’t eat.

00:32:43.880 --> 00:32:47.000
I mean, do you actually
eat vegetables?

00:32:48.680 --> 00:32:51.640
Professor Campbell’s physician
son and daughter-in-law

00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:55.040
tried putting a group of
vegetarians and vegans

00:32:55.040 --> 00:32:57.280
on a whole food,
plant-based diet.

00:32:57.280 --> 00:32:59.360
In eight weeks,
they lost ten pounds,

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:01.280
dropped their LDL
cholesterol 15 points.

00:33:01.280 --> 00:33:02.600
In other words,

00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:09.960
vegans may benefit from eating
a little more plant-based, too.

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:12.680
Now, meat is a problem.

00:33:12.680 --> 00:33:13.880
In terms of lifespan,

00:33:13.880 --> 00:33:17.000
eating a burger may
cut one’s life as short

00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:19.520
as smoking two cigarettes.

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:21.560
So, if it wouldn’t
even occur to us

00:33:21.560 --> 00:33:24.120
to light up before-
and-after lunch,

00:33:24.120 --> 00:33:28.680
maybe we should choose
the bean burrito instead.

00:33:28.680 --> 00:33:31.340
There are certainly ways
to make meat safer, though.

00:33:31.340 --> 00:33:32.080
For example,

00:33:32.080 --> 00:33:34.760
one of the ways whole food,
plant-based diets can help

00:33:34.760 --> 00:33:38.520
is by reducing the
load of gerontotoxins,

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:41.160
age-accelerating toxins,

00:33:41.160 --> 00:33:43.000
such as advanced
glycation end-products,

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:45.760
also known as
glycotoxins, or AGEs,

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:50.080
an acronym intentionally chosen
to emphasize their toxic role

00:33:50.080 --> 00:33:51.640
in age-related disease.

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:54.360
AGEs occur
naturally in animals,

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:58.880
but dry cooking methods can
generate 10 to 100 times more.

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:03.080
When muscle cells
rupture under dry heat,

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:06.560
highly reactive compounds
combine with blood and body sugars

00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:09.440
to form AGEs
within the meat.

00:34:09.440 --> 00:34:11.720
So, even without
cutting down on meat,

00:34:11.720 --> 00:34:14.520
you can significantly cut
down on glycotoxin intake,

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:16.520
just by switching
up cooking methods.

00:34:16.520 --> 00:34:19.120
The safest ways of cooking
meat are moist methods,

00:34:19.120 --> 00:34:22.160
such as boiling, poaching,
steaming, stewing.

00:34:22.160 --> 00:34:24.800
Check out what happens
when people are randomized

00:34:24.800 --> 00:34:28.600
to a meal of fried or broiled
chicken breast and veggies—

00:34:28.600 --> 00:34:30.280
or the same meal with
the same ingredients—

00:34:30.280 --> 00:34:32.200
including the oil—

00:34:32.200 --> 00:34:35.840
but with steamed or
boiled chicken breast instead?

00:34:35.840 --> 00:34:40.600
That single high AGE meal
with the fried or broiled breast

00:34:40.600 --> 00:34:48.080
induced a profound impairment
of artery function within hours.

00:34:48.080 --> 00:34:50.640
The steamed chicken still
impaired artery function,

00:34:50.640 --> 00:34:55.320
but not as much as
the fried or broiled.

00:34:55.320 --> 00:34:58.960
So, we can eat
an “age less” diet

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:01.480
by switching to moist
methods, for example,

00:35:01.480 --> 00:35:06.920
but dietary approaches are said
to have “zero commercial value”

00:35:06.920 --> 00:35:10.760
and hey, stewed chicken
may not be as tasty;

00:35:10.760 --> 00:35:13.560
so, why not
just take a drug

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:18.240
to block AGE absorption
every time we eat?

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:22.840
It does lower your
blood levels of AGEs.

00:35:22.840 --> 00:35:26.360
It’s just activated charcoal,

00:35:26.360 --> 00:35:29.520
like when people are
poisoned or for drug overdoses.

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:33.000
I’m sure our AGEs
would also go down

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:37.800
if we chased our chicken
with a little ipecac, too.

00:35:37.800 --> 00:35:41.040
What about fish?

00:35:41.040 --> 00:35:41.840
Unfortunately,

00:35:41.840 --> 00:35:45.880
our oceans have essentially
become humanity’s sewer.

00:35:45.880 --> 00:35:49.080
Everything eventually
flows into the sea;

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:53.120
so, the consumption
of contaminated seafood

00:35:53.120 --> 00:35:55.360
has become
the main route

00:35:55.360 --> 00:36:00.640
of human exposure
to chemical pollutants.

00:36:00.640 --> 00:36:03.080
Yes, a variety of
foods are affected,

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:06.620
but the number one
source of DDT? Fish.

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:11.400
The number one source
of PCBs? Fish.

00:36:11.400 --> 00:36:15.200
Salmon is the worst
when it comes to PCBs,

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:18.960
followed by canned tuna.

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:22.720
Salmon also has the highest
levels of dioxin-like compounds.

00:36:22.720 --> 00:36:24.720
So, on the one hand,

00:36:24.720 --> 00:36:27.800
fish has long been viewed
as a healthy dietary component

00:36:27.800 --> 00:36:29.480
because of those
long-chain-omega-3’s,

00:36:29.480 --> 00:36:33.240
but on the other hand,
we’re so polluting our world

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:35.000
that we’re now
seeing heavy metals,

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:38.960
pesticides, flame retardants in fish,
forever chemicals,

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:43.000
which may explain why,
despite their omega-3’s,

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:44.360
there’s been a failure

00:36:44.360 --> 00:36:48.280
to consistently observe beneﬁcial
effects of ﬁsh consumption.

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:49.560
So, if we had
a time machine

00:36:49.560 --> 00:36:51.560
to go back before
the industrial revolution,

00:36:51.560 --> 00:36:55.960
that would be one thing,
but now, we have studies like this,

00:36:55.960 --> 00:36:59.440
where mice fed commercially
available farmed salmon ﬁllets

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:02.400
with common levels of
persistent organic pollutants

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:05.880
were found to develop
insulin resistance,

00:37:05.880 --> 00:37:09.080
glucose intolerance,
abdominal obesity, fatty liver,

00:37:09.080 --> 00:37:10.360
chronic low-grade
inﬂammation.

00:37:10.360 --> 00:37:13.320
So, even just background
levels of pollutants

00:37:13.320 --> 00:37:15.400
that might be
presumed to be safe

00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:19.080
could completely counteract
the potential beneﬁts of omega-3s,

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:23.160
in particular,
leading to metabolic issues.

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:26.600
For mercury,
it’s more about brain health.

00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:32.600
Hundreds of thousands of babies born
with brain damage every year,

00:37:32.600 --> 00:37:35.960
seemingly as a consequence
of their mothers

00:37:35.960 --> 00:37:39.440
consuming mercury-contaminated
fish during pregnancy,

00:37:39.440 --> 00:37:42.120
with the resulting
loss of intelligence

00:37:42.120 --> 00:37:50.280
estimated to cause billions of dollars
of lost productivity every year.

00:37:50.280 --> 00:37:53.580
In the aging literature,
there are cases like this:

00:37:53.580 --> 00:37:55.680
a man with progressive
memory loss

00:37:55.680 --> 00:37:59.120
tentatively diagnosed with
Alzheimer’s dementia.

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:05.040
His friends and family assumed
he was nearing the end of his life.

00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:08.640
But a detailed
history revealed

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:11.840
that he had consumed
swordﬁsh once or twice a week.

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:14.880
When the fish was
removed from his diet,

00:38:14.880 --> 00:38:19.760
his mercury levels fell,
and his memory bounced back.

00:38:19.760 --> 00:38:21.560
No more dementia.

00:38:21.560 --> 00:38:25.320
So, it seemed he didn’t
have Alzheimer’s after all,

00:38:25.320 --> 00:38:29.600
but rather mercury poisoning
from a handful of monthly meals

00:38:29.600 --> 00:38:32.720
of contaminated fish.

00:38:32.720 --> 00:38:36.640
You can see the same
remarkable reversals with hair loss.

00:38:36.640 --> 00:38:37.720
Here’s a common story:

00:38:37.720 --> 00:38:40.800
a perimenopausal woman
seeks help for hair loss.

00:38:40.800 --> 00:38:43.440
Blood tests indicate
elevated mercury levels.

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:44.400
And no wonder,

00:38:44.400 --> 00:38:47.240
because she had
a diet high in tuna.

00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:49.960
But within two months
of stopping fish,

00:38:49.960 --> 00:38:53.120
her hair started
to grow back,

00:38:53.120 --> 00:38:57.840
and eventually her
hair regrew completely.

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:01.560
So, doctors should consider
screening for mercury toxicity

00:39:01.560 --> 00:39:03.080
when they see hair loss,

00:39:03.080 --> 00:39:05.720
since there’s something
we can do about it.

00:39:05.720 --> 00:39:08.400
Instructing patients to
reduce fish intake

00:39:08.400 --> 00:39:10.200
could offer relief
of symptoms

00:39:10.200 --> 00:39:11.560
and uncover
dietary habits

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:16.200
that may be a source of
heavy-metal-induced hair loss.

00:39:16.200 --> 00:39:17.600
(Though, admittedly,

00:39:17.600 --> 00:39:22.120
sometimes heavy metal
can lead to too much hair.)

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:31.720
What about alcohol?
That’s from plants!

00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:35.920
Sorry to be Dr. Buzzkill,

00:39:35.920 --> 00:39:41.080
but alcohol appears to be humanity’s
7th leading cause of death and disability.

00:39:41.080 --> 00:39:47.520
The safest level
of drinking is none.

00:39:47.520 --> 00:39:50.320
But wait a second,
what about the famous J curve,

00:39:50.320 --> 00:39:54.080
where light to moderate drinkers
appear to have lower mortality rates

00:39:54.080 --> 00:39:56.400
than the teetotalers
at zero drinks a day?

00:39:57.200 --> 00:40:01.760
This, may just be an artifact
of the systemic misclassification

00:40:01.760 --> 00:40:05.720
of former drinkers
as lifelong abstainers.

00:40:05.720 --> 00:40:10.040
This leads to the so-called
“sick-quitter” effect,

00:40:10.040 --> 00:40:12.720
where the reason a person
may be a nondrinker

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:14.680
is because drinking
made them sick,

00:40:14.680 --> 00:40:19.720
thereby making current drinkers
look good in comparison.

00:40:19.720 --> 00:40:24.120
It’s the same reason studies
can find higher mortality

00:40:24.120 --> 00:40:26.400
among those who
quit smoking

00:40:26.400 --> 00:40:28.440
compared to those
who continue to smoke.

00:40:28.440 --> 00:40:30.280
It’s not that abstention
led to poor health,

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:34.120
but poor health rather
led to abstention.

00:40:34.120 --> 00:40:37.840
When researchers went back
and controlled for the error

00:40:37.840 --> 00:40:43.480
of misclassifying former drinkers,
the J curve disappeared.

00:40:43.480 --> 00:40:48.440
No more apparent benefit
to moderate drinking.

00:40:48.440 --> 00:40:52.320
In other words,
compared to true abstainers,

00:40:52.320 --> 00:40:55.800
the more alcohol,
the more disease,

00:40:55.800 --> 00:41:02.040
with no apparent protection
at low levels of consumption.

00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:04.480
Even red wine?

00:41:04.480 --> 00:41:05.280
Unfortunately,

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:09.160
interventional trials show that
wine can cause inflammation

00:41:09.160 --> 00:41:12.280
within hours of consumption,
both red and white—

00:41:12.280 --> 00:41:14.920
significantly more than just
like drinking sugar water,

00:41:14.920 --> 00:41:17.240
and you get a surge of
fat in your bloodstream

00:41:17.240 --> 00:41:20.880
that you don’t get drinking
“dealcoholized red wine”

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:23.840
(the same wine with
the alcohol taken out).

00:41:23.840 --> 00:41:25.840
What about just
taking supplements

00:41:25.840 --> 00:41:30.120
of the red wine
compound resveratrol?

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:31.320
Oh, you mean the supplement

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:36.800
found to nearly triple the loss of
brain tissue compared to placebo?

00:41:36.800 --> 00:41:41.520
No, thank you.

00:41:41.520 --> 00:41:44.680
The healthiest beverages
are probably water and tea.

00:41:44.680 --> 00:41:46.920
Increasing tea consumption
three cups (720 ml) a day

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:51.520
may decrease the risk of premature
death from all causes by 24 percent.

00:41:51.520 --> 00:41:53.800
This applied to both
green and black tea,

00:41:53.800 --> 00:41:56.240
though green may
have a slight advantage.

00:41:56.920 --> 00:42:02.280
Part of this protection may be
a lower risk of dying of pneumonia.

00:42:02.280 --> 00:42:04.600
There have been five
randomized, controlled trials

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:06.440
showing those
randomized to green tea

00:42:06.440 --> 00:42:07.520
are about a
third less likely

00:42:07.520 --> 00:42:09.920
to come down with
the flu, for example.

00:42:09.920 --> 00:42:14.120
Within just one hour of drinking
a single cup (200 ml) of green tea,

00:42:14.120 --> 00:42:15.800
we can significantly cut down

00:42:15.800 --> 00:42:18.640
on the rate of DNA
damage in our cells,

00:42:18.640 --> 00:42:23.600
because green tea significantly boosts
the activity of a DNA repair enzyme

00:42:23.600 --> 00:42:26.240
in our body that can
fix DNA damage.

00:42:26.240 --> 00:42:27.920
And within a month,

00:42:27.920 --> 00:42:30.120
drinking two small
daily cups (400 ml) of green tea

00:42:30.120 --> 00:42:34.320
can improve DNA resistance to
free radical damage in the first place,

00:42:34.320 --> 00:42:38.280
indicating that green tea has
significant genoprotective effects,

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:42.400
or DNA protecting effects.

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:45.080
Tons more about individual foods
and beverages in the book.

00:42:45.080 --> 00:42:50.880
But let’s move on to Part Three,
where I get into the nitty gritty.

00:42:50.880 --> 00:42:55.560
What can we do to protect our
bones, bowels, bladder, and circulation?

00:42:55.560 --> 00:42:58.280
Our hair, hearing,
and hormone balance?

00:42:58.280 --> 00:43:00.320
Immune function,
joint health?

00:43:00.320 --> 00:43:01.320
Our minds and our muscles?

00:43:01.320 --> 00:43:02.560
Sex life and skin?

00:43:02.560 --> 00:43:06.520
Our teeth, vision, and finally,
our dignity in death.

00:43:06.520 --> 00:43:09.960
Let me just share
a few quick pearls.

00:43:09.960 --> 00:43:10.960
For example,

00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:18.920
did you know that 85 percent
of bone-related fracture risk

00:43:18.920 --> 00:43:23.040
has nothing to do with
your bone mineral density?

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:26.520
That’s just been drilled
into our heads by big pharma

00:43:26.520 --> 00:43:29.560
and a billion-dollar bone
density screening industry.

00:43:29.560 --> 00:43:35.480
Fractures are primarily due
to falling, not osteoporosis.

00:43:35.480 --> 00:43:39.280
So, it’s the age-related muscle
loss and impaired balance

00:43:39.280 --> 00:43:41.000
that may be breaking
most of our bones—

00:43:41.000 --> 00:43:42.800
which is good news,

00:43:42.800 --> 00:43:45.200
because there’s something
we can do something about it.

00:43:45.200 --> 00:43:46.709
Seven randomized
controlled trials

00:43:46.709 --> 00:43:47.540
found that strength
and balance training

00:43:47.540 --> 00:43:49.560
can cut fracture
rates nearly in half—

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:50.800
and that’s just
during the trial.

00:43:50.800 --> 00:43:54.560
In the five years after this
one-year study ended,

00:43:54.560 --> 00:43:57.560
those who had previously
been randomized

00:43:57.560 --> 00:43:59.840
to the combined strength
and balance group,

00:43:59.840 --> 00:44:03.360
suffered 74 percent
fewer fractures.

00:44:03.360 --> 00:44:09.800
That’s far more effective than
osteoporosis drugs like Fosamax

00:44:09.800 --> 00:44:13.880
that can cause fractures—
atypical femoral fractures,

00:44:13.880 --> 00:44:16.080
in as many as
1 in 300 users.

00:44:16.080 --> 00:44:18.480
They are called “atypical,”

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:21.120
because they occur
not after a fall or trauma

00:44:21.120 --> 00:44:24.000
but just during routine
activities, like walking,

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:27.480
twisting at the hip,
or even just standing still.

00:44:27.480 --> 00:44:33.520
Thanks to the drug,
your femur, your thigh bone,

00:44:33.520 --> 00:44:41.040
the biggest bone in your body—
just cracks in half.

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:47.000
Too cruel an irony from a drug
that’s supposed to protect your bones.

00:44:47.000 --> 00:44:53.520
This is what it looks
like on x-ray. Ouch!

00:44:53.520 --> 00:44:54.320
After bones,

00:44:54.320 --> 00:44:57.320
I talk about preserving
your bowel function

00:44:57.320 --> 00:45:01.240
in chapter number two.

00:45:02.280 --> 00:45:04.080
Randomize constipated
diabetics

00:45:04.080 --> 00:45:07.000
to either cookies containing
a tablespoon (20 g) a day

00:45:07.000 --> 00:45:07.880
of ground flaxseeds

00:45:07.880 --> 00:45:10.800
or flax-free placebo
cookies for 12 weeks

00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:14.800
and not only does the flax
improve constipation symptoms

00:45:14.800 --> 00:45:17.920
but resulted in an eight-pound
weight loss over placebo,

00:45:17.920 --> 00:45:20.040
25-point lower
blood sugars,

00:45:20.040 --> 00:45:25.000
an astounding drop in hemoglobin A1c,
and 17-point lower LDL cholesterol.

00:45:25.000 --> 00:45:28.960
All for about 10 cents a day
of ground flaxseeds.

00:45:28.960 --> 00:45:32.040
For a head-to-head test
between flaxseeds and psyllium,

00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:33.400
sold as Metamucil,

00:45:33.400 --> 00:45:37.040
a third cookie group was added
with 10 grams of psyllium,

00:45:37.040 --> 00:45:41.480
and the flaxseed beat out the
psyllium for constipation relief,

00:45:41.480 --> 00:45:44.800
weight, blood sugars,
and cholesterol—

00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:48.000
and, is about four
times cheaper.

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:52.840
Flaxseeds were also directly compared
to the prescription laxative lactulose

00:45:52.840 --> 00:45:55.880
and worked better as well.

00:45:57.400 --> 00:45:58.620
Bladder function!

00:45:58.620 --> 00:46:02.640
The prevalence of overactive
bladder syndrome increases with age,

00:46:02.640 --> 00:46:04.840
reaching about
one in three.

00:46:04.840 --> 00:46:10.680
No wonder bladder-relaxing drugs
are a multibillion-dollar industry,

00:46:10.680 --> 00:46:12.840
yet may only reduce
bathroom breaks

00:46:12.840 --> 00:46:17.600
by a half a pee a day
compared to placebo.

00:46:17.600 --> 00:46:22.480
But a quarter teaspoon (500 mg) a day
of dried cranberry powder

00:46:22.480 --> 00:46:24.600
worked nearly
four times better,

00:46:24.600 --> 00:46:28.160
about two fewer daily trips
to the bathroom over placebo,

00:46:28.160 --> 00:46:30.840
and that’s without any of
the drug’s side effects:

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:33.120
dry mouth, constipation,
sedation, impaired cognition,

00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:34.440
rapid heartbeat,
urinary retention,

00:46:35.000 --> 00:46:37.520
and the visual disturbances
that lead almost two-thirds

00:46:37.520 --> 00:46:39.280
to stop taking the drugs,

00:46:39.280 --> 00:46:46.120
whereas cranberries
are just tangy.

00:46:46.120 --> 00:46:49.520
Urinary incontinence is a common
problem among older adults,

00:46:49.520 --> 00:46:52.160
particularly women,
thanks to childbirth.

00:46:52.160 --> 00:46:58.080
Systemic menopausal hormone therapy
like Premarin actually makes it worse,

00:46:58.080 --> 00:47:01.280
but local, vaginal
estrogen may help.

00:47:01.280 --> 00:47:05.200
What works five times better
than estrogen cream though?

00:47:05.200 --> 00:47:09.200
Kegel exercises,
pelvic floor exercises,

00:47:09.200 --> 00:47:21.880
as I detail in the section
“Jacked in the Box.”

00:47:21.880 --> 00:47:26.600
For men, urinary issues are more
an enlarged prostate problem,

00:47:26.600 --> 00:47:30.760
having to get up
multiple times at night,

00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:35.800
wee hours of the night.

00:47:38.280 --> 00:47:41.840
Cranberries to the
rescue there again.

00:47:41.840 --> 00:47:45.520
Even just an eighth of a teaspoon (250 mg)
a day of powdered cranberries

00:47:45.520 --> 00:47:48.480
can significantly improve
prostate symptoms.

00:47:48.480 --> 00:47:51.040
You can buy cranberry
powder in bulk

00:47:51.040 --> 00:47:54.960
at a cost of less than
a penny per day.

00:47:54.960 --> 00:47:57.000
What about
saw palmetto,

00:47:57.000 --> 00:48:00.720
the most common herbal supplement
used for prostate symptoms?

00:48:00.720 --> 00:48:03.400
Based on dozens of randomized,
controlled trials

00:48:03.400 --> 00:48:08.400
involving nearly 5,000 men:
no clinical benefits.

00:48:08.400 --> 00:48:11.400
But pumpkin seeds work.

00:48:11.400 --> 00:48:14.560
More than a thousand men with
prostate symptoms were randomized

00:48:14.560 --> 00:48:18.680
to either about a tablespoon (15 g) a day
of plain pumpkin seed kernels,

00:48:18.680 --> 00:48:22.400
a proprietary pumpkin seed
extract, or a placebo.

00:48:22.400 --> 00:48:27.400
The study was funded by the drug
company that made the supplement,

00:48:27.400 --> 00:48:31.680
but the supplement
totally flopped,

00:48:31.680 --> 00:48:36.000
while the pumpkin seeds
themselves worked.

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:42.200
Pumpkin seed oil was pitted against
the leading drug for hair loss in women.

00:48:42.200 --> 00:48:47.040
A little less than a quarter teaspoon (1 ml)
applied to the scalp once a day

00:48:47.040 --> 00:48:52.520
versus a dose of the drug,
and they both worked,

00:48:52.520 --> 00:48:55.240
but the drug worked better.

00:48:55.240 --> 00:49:00.040
Rosemary oil, however,
may be more closely matched.

00:49:00.040 --> 00:49:04.280
A hundred balding men were randomized
to twice a day minoxidil like Rogaine

00:49:04.280 --> 00:49:05.600
versus a rosemary lotion.

00:49:05.600 --> 00:49:06.640
It took six months,

00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:11.040
but significant comparable improvement
in hair counts in both groups,

00:49:11.040 --> 00:49:13.240
with no significant difference
between the two.

00:49:13.240 --> 00:49:17.080
The rosemary lotion appeared
to work as well as the drug.

00:49:17.080 --> 00:49:20.280
If you want to
give it a DIY try,

00:49:20.280 --> 00:49:24.200
you can premix about 10 drops
of rosemary essential oil

00:49:24.200 --> 00:49:26.720
to each fluid ounce (30 ml)
of your favorite lotion

00:49:26.720 --> 00:49:31.240
and rub a quarter teaspoon (1.23 ml)
into your scalp twice a day.

00:49:31.240 --> 00:49:34.200
Cost-wise,
that much rosemary oil

00:49:34.200 --> 00:49:38.560
would come out to be
about a penny per week.

00:49:38.560 --> 00:49:42.560
Let’s move on from hair
highlights to hormones.

00:49:42.560 --> 00:49:45.360
I touched upon how
menopausal hormone therapy

00:49:45.360 --> 00:49:51.440
can increase the risk of urinary
incontinence, but that’s not all.

00:49:51.440 --> 00:49:54.400
If a thousand
postmenopausal women

00:49:54.400 --> 00:49:57.640
take the standard hormone
regimen for ten years,

00:49:57.640 --> 00:50:03.200
you’d not only expect 876
extra cases of urinary incontinence

00:50:03.200 --> 00:50:05.560
that they would not
have got otherwise,

00:50:05.560 --> 00:50:10.520
but nine more cases of
invasive breast cancer,

00:50:10.520 --> 00:50:12.120
eight more cases
of heart disease,

00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:14.280
probably 22 more
cases of dementia,

00:50:14.280 --> 00:50:16.440
21 more cases of
gallbladder disease,

00:50:16.440 --> 00:50:19.200
nine more strokes,
and 21 more blood clots—

00:50:19.200 --> 00:50:25.280
though not a single
partridge nor pear tree.

00:50:25.280 --> 00:50:28.160
However, hormone
therapy is highly effective

00:50:28.160 --> 00:50:30.360
in decreasing
menopausal hot flashes.

00:50:30.360 --> 00:50:34.600
Is there any way to keep cool
without the clots and cancer?

00:50:34.600 --> 00:50:39.280
In the U.S., menopausal hot
flashes are considered inevitable,

00:50:39.280 --> 00:50:46.120
but there isn’t even a word
for hot flash in Japanese.

00:50:46.120 --> 00:50:50.560
This may be due to their
greater consumption of soy foods,

00:50:50.560 --> 00:50:54.120
but you don’t really know
until you put it to the test.

00:50:54.120 --> 00:50:55.880
Harvard’s Center of Excellence
in Women’s Health

00:50:55.880 --> 00:50:58.760
funded a randomized,
crossover trial

00:50:58.760 --> 00:51:01.600
of a half a cup (118 ml) of
unsalted soy nuts a day

00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:07.360
and achieved about a 40 to 45
percent reduction in hot flashes

00:51:07.360 --> 00:51:10.680
within two weeks compared
to the control group.

00:51:10.680 --> 00:51:15.560
Given that a plant-based
diet may also be effective

00:51:15.560 --> 00:51:18.280
for managing
menopausal symptoms,

00:51:18.280 --> 00:51:27.440
researchers decided to combine the two:
a plant-based diet plus whole soybeans.

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:34.080
In fact, two randomized controlled trials
found that plant-based nutrition

00:51:34.080 --> 00:51:37.080
with a daily half cup (86 g) of
cooked whole soybeans

00:51:37.080 --> 00:51:41.520
can reduce the number of serious
hot flashes by 84 to 88 percent

00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:43.160
within twelve weeks.

00:51:43.160 --> 00:51:46.280
Overall, most randomized to
the plant-based bean group

00:51:46.280 --> 00:51:48.480
ended up free of
moderate-to-severe hot flashes

00:51:48.480 --> 00:51:54.000
compared to about 95 percent
still suffering in the control group.

00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:55.360
Lots of other
things can help.

00:51:55.360 --> 00:51:56.800
Whole fennel seeds,

00:51:56.800 --> 00:51:59.440
powdered into capsules
to pit them against placebo

00:51:59.440 --> 00:52:02.440
in a double-blind trial significantly
improved menopausal symptoms

00:52:02.440 --> 00:52:04.880
at a dose of just
a teaspoon (2 g) a day.

00:52:04.880 --> 00:52:09.920
Fenugreek may also help at a dose
of one and a half teaspoons (6 g) a day,

00:52:09.920 --> 00:52:14.720
though not as much as hormones,
but without the side effects.

00:52:14.720 --> 00:52:20.360
Though fenugreek can make your
pee smell like maple syrup,

00:52:20.360 --> 00:52:25.280
sounds like a bonus!

00:52:26.160 --> 00:52:28.760
The nice thing about
studying spices

00:52:28.760 --> 00:52:31.960
is that entire servings
can be stuffed into a pill,

00:52:31.960 --> 00:52:34.040
to pit them
against placebo.

00:52:34.040 --> 00:52:37.120
A quarter teaspoon (1 g) of
black cumin powder a day

00:52:37.120 --> 00:52:39.640
led to significant
improvements in memory

00:52:39.640 --> 00:52:45.440
and other tests of cognition within
nine weeks compared to placebo.

00:52:45.440 --> 00:52:47.720
Black sesame seeds—

00:52:47.720 --> 00:52:52.000
less than a teaspoon (2.52 g) a day
of ground black sesame seeds—

00:52:52.000 --> 00:52:54.320
stuffed into capsules
against placebo,

00:52:54.320 --> 00:52:58.680
drove down systolic blood pressures
by eight points within a month.

00:52:58.680 --> 00:53:05.000
If sustained, that alone could decrease
the risk of stroke by about a quarter.

00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:07.720
What else can we powder?
Strawberries!

00:53:07.720 --> 00:53:09.760
Osteoarthritis patients
were randomized

00:53:09.760 --> 00:53:12.480
to two ounces (50 g) of freeze-
dried strawberry powder

00:53:12.480 --> 00:53:16.880
versus a fake, strawberry-flavored
and colored placebo powder,

00:53:16.880 --> 00:53:19.360
and, compared to placebo,

00:53:19.360 --> 00:53:23.880
the real strawberries signiﬁcantly
decreased constant pain,

00:53:23.880 --> 00:53:26.440
intermittent pain,
and total knee pain,

00:53:26.440 --> 00:53:30.960
and improved disability
and overall quality of life.

00:53:30.960 --> 00:53:36.760
Osteoarthritis is the most frequent cause
of physical disability among older adults,

00:53:36.760 --> 00:53:38.240
yet how do we treat it?

00:53:38.240 --> 00:53:43.400
Acetaminophen—Tylenol is
considered the first-line pain killer,

00:53:43.400 --> 00:53:47.000
but guess what:
it doesn’t work.

00:53:47.000 --> 00:53:47.920
I mean—it works,

00:53:47.920 --> 00:53:50.720
just not much better
than a sugar pill,

00:53:50.720 --> 00:53:55.640
but at least it’s not going
to make matters worse,

00:53:55.640 --> 00:53:59.680
like other things we doctors have
cooked up, like steroid injections,

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:03.360
which actively worsen
joint deterioration

00:54:03.360 --> 00:54:06.680
and offer no greater
pain relief than placebos.

00:54:06.680 --> 00:54:08.600
Or arthroscopic surgery,

00:54:08.600 --> 00:54:10.560
which may end up
tripling our risk

00:54:10.560 --> 00:54:14.160
of ending up having to get
a total full knee replacement.

00:54:14.160 --> 00:54:18.880
I mean so, even if the strawberry
study is some total fluke,

00:54:18.880 --> 00:54:24.320
what’s the worst that can
happen, tastier smoothies?

00:54:24.320 --> 00:54:27.320
And less than a quarter of a teaspoon
(~1.25 ml) of ground ginger a day

00:54:27.320 --> 00:54:30.360
can beat out placebo
for joint pain.

00:54:30.360 --> 00:54:35.320
Researchers conclude
ginger, "therefore,

00:54:35.320 --> 00:54:40.600
is recommended as a safe
drug for these patients,”

00:54:40.600 --> 00:54:46.360
but by “drug” they just mean
a couple pinches of the ground ginger

00:54:46.360 --> 00:54:49.600
you can buy
at any store.

00:54:49.600 --> 00:54:53.120
There was even a
study on topical ginger—

00:54:53.120 --> 00:54:57.120
at least, when applied
to the scrotum.

00:54:57.120 --> 00:55:01.760
Paper-thin slices of ginger
applied over the inflamed testicles,

00:55:01.760 --> 00:55:08.960
and the researchers
were, on the ball,

00:55:08.960 --> 00:55:14.360
healing nearly three times
faster than the control group.

00:55:14.360 --> 00:55:19.040
There have been more than a hundred
randomized, controlled trials on ginger,

00:55:19.040 --> 00:55:21.920
shown to help with
nausea and vomiting

00:55:21.920 --> 00:55:25.000
and everything from
COVID to PMS.

00:55:26.600 --> 00:55:29.800
Heavy periods,
painful periods,

00:55:29.800 --> 00:55:33.360
migraine headaches,
multiple sclerosis,

00:55:33.360 --> 00:55:35.040
and the list goes on.

00:55:35.040 --> 00:55:40.600
Bottom line: “Mounting evidence suggests
ginger can promote healthy aging.”

00:55:40.600 --> 00:55:43.120
What about garlic?

00:55:43.120 --> 00:55:50.160
Clinically proven to protect against
those who vant to suck your blood.

00:55:50.160 --> 00:55:51.120
Literally.

00:55:51.120 --> 00:55:54.440
A hundred marines were randomized
to a clove a day or placebo,

00:55:54.440 --> 00:56:00.800
and those on garlic had
significantly fewer tick bites.

00:56:00.800 --> 00:56:07.800
Though sadly, garlic does not appear
to help against other bloodsuckers.

00:56:07.800 --> 00:56:10.400
So far, I’ve touched on
preserving your bones, bowels,

00:56:10.400 --> 00:56:12.360
bladder, hair,
hormones, and joints,

00:56:12.360 --> 00:56:18.280
but preserving your mind is
the largest chapter in the book,

00:56:18.280 --> 00:56:21.600
because dementia is one of our
most pressing public health problems

00:56:21.600 --> 00:56:26.520
and the most feared
condition of later life.

00:56:26.520 --> 00:56:29.520
There’s a common misconception
that we have no control

00:56:29.520 --> 00:56:31.240
over whether we
develop dementia,

00:56:31.240 --> 00:56:35.880
but the good news is that although
Alzheimer’s may be incurable,

00:56:35.880 --> 00:56:40.720
at least it is preventable.

00:56:40.720 --> 00:56:43.000
There is an
emerging consensus,

00:56:43.000 --> 00:56:51.520
that “what is good for our hearts
is also good for our heads,”

00:56:51.520 --> 00:56:54.480
because clogging of the
arteries inside the brain

00:56:54.480 --> 00:56:57.480
with atherosclerotic plaque
is thought to play a role

00:56:57.480 --> 00:57:01.080
in the development of
Alzheimer’s dementia.

00:57:01.080 --> 00:57:05.240
This is what our cerebral
arteries should look like:

00:57:05.240 --> 00:57:10.200
open, clean, allowing blood
to flow throughout our brain.

00:57:10.200 --> 00:57:17.800
This is what atherosclerosis
in our head looks like.

00:57:17.800 --> 00:57:20.040
Clogged with cholesterol,
closing off our arteries—

00:57:20.040 --> 00:57:22.760
clamping down
on blood flow.

00:57:22.760 --> 00:57:32.440
What kind of brain arteries
do you want in your head?

00:57:32.440 --> 00:57:37.880
Too much cholesterol in our blood is
unanimously recognized to be a risk factor

00:57:37.880 --> 00:57:40.400
for the development of
Alzheimer’s disease.

00:57:40.400 --> 00:57:43.480
Those with a total
cholesterol of 225 mg/dL or more

00:57:43.480 --> 00:57:46.280
may have nearly
25 times the odds

00:57:46.280 --> 00:57:51.280
of ending up with amyloid plaques
in their brain 10 to 15 years later.

00:57:51.280 --> 00:57:56.800
Cholesterol explains how the Alzheimer's
gene APOE4 ravages the brain.

00:57:56.800 --> 00:58:04.960
After all, APOE is the primary
cholesterol carrier in the brain.

00:58:04.960 --> 00:58:10.760
LDL cholesterol in those with
bad gene variants of APOE

00:58:10.760 --> 00:58:12.720
averages 40 points higher,

00:58:12.720 --> 00:58:16.120
but switch people to a
diet lower in animal fat

00:58:16.120 --> 00:58:19.480
and that cholesterol difference
can be effectively smoothed out.

00:58:19.480 --> 00:58:23.640
So, diet can trump genetics.

00:58:23.640 --> 00:58:27.080
This may explain the
so-called Nigerian paradox,

00:58:27.600 --> 00:58:31.640
where they have among the
highest rates of the Alzheimer's gene

00:58:31.640 --> 00:58:36.720
but some of the lowest rates
of Alzheimer’s disease.

00:58:36.720 --> 00:58:37.840
How is that possible?

00:58:37.840 --> 00:58:42.200
Genes load the gun,
but lifestyle pulls the trigger.

00:58:42.200 --> 00:58:45.600
The paradox may be explained
by their low cholesterol levels,

00:58:45.600 --> 00:58:49.760
probably because of their
diets low in animal fat.

00:58:49.760 --> 00:58:53.040
Human beings may
have evolved

00:58:53.040 --> 00:58:57.480
to maintain an LDL cholesterol
level of around 25 mg/dL,

00:58:57.480 --> 00:59:04.240
but the average in the Western
world is approximately 120 mg/dL.

00:59:04.240 --> 00:59:08.400
No wonder heart disease is
the leading cause of death

00:59:08.400 --> 00:59:12.720
in high income countries,
and dementia is killer #2.

00:59:14.160 --> 00:59:17.400
That helps explain why the
so-called Alzheimer’s gene

00:59:17.400 --> 00:59:21.360
is the single most important gene
when it comes to longevity, too.

00:59:21.360 --> 00:59:22.760
Which is good news,

00:59:22.760 --> 00:59:27.040
because even if we’ve been
dealt some bad genetic cards,

00:59:27.040 --> 00:59:31.400
we still may be able to
reshuffle the deck with diet.

00:59:31.400 --> 00:59:35.760
So, in terms of dietary guidelines
for the prevention of Alzheimer’s,

00:59:35.760 --> 00:59:39.880
we should center our diets
around vegetables, legumes,

00:59:39.880 --> 00:59:41.680
fruits, and whole grains.

00:59:41.680 --> 00:59:46.000
In other words, the dietary
pillar of lifestyle medicine:

00:59:46.000 --> 00:59:48.680
whole food,
plant-based, nutrition.

00:59:48.680 --> 00:59:54.760
Or even simpler, plants,
plants, and more plants.

00:59:54.760 --> 00:59:56.680
Any plants in particular?

00:59:56.680 --> 01:00:01.000
Note that the directors of Loma Linda’s
Alzheimer’s Prevention Program

01:00:01.000 --> 01:00:03.720
single out berries
and greens.

01:00:03.720 --> 01:00:05.240
Eating strawberries
and spinach

01:00:05.240 --> 01:00:08.600
can mitigate age-related
cognitive decline in rats,

01:00:08.600 --> 01:00:10.760
but what about in people?

01:00:10.760 --> 01:00:15.360
When the cognition of hundreds
of twins was followed over a decade,

01:00:15.360 --> 01:00:18.880
consuming the berry pigments in less
than a quarter cup (23 g) of blueberries a day

01:00:18.880 --> 01:00:20.680
or about a daily
cup (138 g) of strawberries

01:00:20.680 --> 01:00:25.920
was associated with slowed
cognitive aging by about four years,

01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:28.520
and nearly all of the randomized
controlled trials of blueberries

01:00:28.520 --> 01:00:33.640
and cognitive performance found
improvements in at least one brain domain,

01:00:33.640 --> 01:00:37.840
perhaps, because blueberry
consumption can improve blood flow

01:00:37.840 --> 01:00:41.040
to certain critical
regions of the brain.

01:00:41.040 --> 01:00:46.000
Now, the one study that flopped
mixed the berries with milk,

01:00:46.000 --> 01:00:48.880
and we know the
addition of milk

01:00:48.880 --> 01:00:51.560
prevents the artery
protective effects of tea

01:00:51.560 --> 01:00:54.640
and prevents the
bump in antioxidants

01:00:54.640 --> 01:00:57.240
that you’d normally get
eating dark chocolate

01:00:57.240 --> 01:00:59.480
and impairs
the absorption

01:00:59.480 --> 01:01:03.120
of the autophagy-activating
compounds in coffee.

01:01:03.120 --> 01:01:06.480
What about mixing
berries and cream?

01:01:06.480 --> 01:01:10.480
The antioxidant activity of blueberries
is not just impaired by milk.

01:01:10.480 --> 01:01:11.840
Check this out.

01:01:11.840 --> 01:01:16.880
Researchers found that the total
antioxidant capacity of our bloodstream

01:01:16.880 --> 01:01:17.920
(measured in two
different ways)

01:01:17.920 --> 01:01:22.800
shoots up within an hour of eating
a cup-and-a-half (200 g) of blueberries with water

01:01:22.800 --> 01:01:25.560
and remains elevated
five hours later.

01:01:25.560 --> 01:01:30.160
With milk, maybe one
would expect less of a bump?

01:01:30.160 --> 01:01:34.040
But in fact, they ended up even
worse than when they started.

01:01:34.040 --> 01:01:36.240
After eating a whole
bowl of blueberries,

01:01:36.240 --> 01:01:40.000
they ended up with less
antioxidant capacity in their body—

01:01:40.000 --> 01:01:43.400
because they ate
blueberries with milk.

01:01:43.400 --> 01:01:47.840
One reason greens may be referred
to as an anti-Alzheimer’s plant

01:01:47.840 --> 01:01:52.560
is because dark green leafy vegetables
can also improve blood flow in the brain

01:01:52.560 --> 01:01:53.880
in interventional trials.

01:01:53.880 --> 01:01:56.160
Randomize older adults
to the greens pigments

01:01:56.160 --> 01:01:58.800
found in about a cup (12 mg) a
day’s worth of cooked kale

01:01:58.800 --> 01:02:02.920
and see significant improvements
in complex attention, cognitive flexibility.

01:02:02.920 --> 01:02:05.000
And those randomized
to even just a half cup

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:08.600
of kale’s worth of the cruciferous
compound sulforaphane

01:02:08.600 --> 01:02:12.520
got significant improvements in
processing speed and working memory.

01:02:12.520 --> 01:02:16.400
Here’s the rate of cognitive
decline in elderly men and women

01:02:16.400 --> 01:02:19.640
eating a serving a
day of green leafies,

01:02:19.640 --> 01:02:24.680
compared to those only eating
greens about once every ten days.

01:02:24.680 --> 01:02:26.960
Are you sitting down?

01:02:26.960 --> 01:02:28.960
“The rates of decline
among those

01:02:28.960 --> 01:02:31.880
who consumed 1 to 2
servings of greens a day

01:02:31.880 --> 01:02:37.400
was the equivalent to
being 11 years younger… .”

01:02:37.400 --> 01:02:43.080
So, now are you sitting
down to a big salad?

01:02:43.080 --> 01:02:45.240
Greens come up in
chapter after chapter.

01:02:45.240 --> 01:02:49.640
What’s the recommendation for preventing
age-related macular degeneration,

01:02:49.640 --> 01:02:52.560
our leading cause
of blindness?

01:02:52.560 --> 01:02:56.400
Or even treating it
in its earliest stages?

01:02:56.400 --> 01:03:02.400
Consume a diet high
in green leafy vegetables.

01:03:02.400 --> 01:03:04.720
We’re talkin’ two to
three servings a day,

01:03:04.720 --> 01:03:07.520
so at least greens at
every lunch and supper,

01:03:07.520 --> 01:03:11.080
with bonus points for sneaking them
into breakfast in, say, a green smoothie.

01:03:11.080 --> 01:03:15.520
In fact, blending greens
can triple the bioavailability

01:03:15.520 --> 01:03:19.560
of a key, vision-
protecting nutrient.

01:03:19.560 --> 01:03:24.560
The main reason greens make
it into in my Anti-Aging 8 though,

01:03:24.560 --> 01:03:29.880
is because of the nitrates in
a serving or two of cooked greens

01:03:29.880 --> 01:03:34.040
can slow our metabolic
rate for the same reason,

01:03:34.040 --> 01:03:38.440
a little shot of beet juice can help
free divers hold their breath longer,

01:03:38.440 --> 01:03:42.180
because it improves the
efficiency of our mitochondria,

01:03:42.180 --> 01:03:44.560
the little power
plants in our cells,

01:03:44.560 --> 01:03:48.800
allowing us to effectively extract
more energy from each breath.

01:03:48.800 --> 01:03:52.200
Another dietary intervention that has
a similar effect on slowing metabolism

01:03:52.200 --> 01:03:53.560
is calorie restriction.

01:03:53.560 --> 01:03:57.400
The candle that burns half
as bright burns twice as long.

01:03:57.400 --> 01:04:03.800
But instead of starving all the time,
you can just eat a big salad.

01:04:03.800 --> 01:04:05.360
What else can
greens do?

01:04:05.360 --> 01:04:07.200
In the Harvard Nurses’
Health Study,

01:04:07.200 --> 01:04:09.440
the consumption of leafy
greens appeared to protect

01:04:09.440 --> 01:04:11.760
against the
development of frailty,

01:04:11.760 --> 01:04:16.160
and when put to the test in a randomized,
double-blind, placebo-controlled trial,

01:04:16.160 --> 01:04:18.640
the equivalent of two
thirds of a cup (158 ml) of spinach

01:04:18.640 --> 01:04:22.840
led to a significant boost in
muscle strength, muscle quality,

01:04:22.840 --> 01:04:25.640
and muscle mass in
older men and women.

01:04:25.640 --> 01:04:28.880
A single cup (240 ml) of cooked greens
worth of vegetable nitrates

01:04:28.880 --> 01:04:34.520
significantly boosted maximal power
and velocity in quads so much

01:04:34.520 --> 01:04:36.440
it was “functionally equivalent,

01:04:36.440 --> 01:04:41.880
to acutely reversing the effects
of several decades of aging.”

01:04:41.880 --> 01:04:46.200
A single cup (240 ml)
of cooked greens!

01:04:46.200 --> 01:04:49.640
Of course, when most people think
about maintaining muscle mass into old age,

01:04:49.640 --> 01:04:53.920
they don’t think spinach,
they think protein.

01:04:53.920 --> 01:04:59.240
But if you put together all of
the randomized, controlled trials

01:04:59.240 --> 01:05:04.760
of adding extra protein to the
diets of older men and women,

01:05:04.760 --> 01:05:12.680
you find no evidence that adding protein
increases muscle mass or strength.

01:05:12.680 --> 01:05:17.520
Yeah, but what if you have sarcopenia,
excessive age-related muscle loss?

01:05:17.520 --> 01:05:21.080
Surely then,
protein helps, right?

01:05:21.080 --> 01:05:22.000
No.

01:05:22.000 --> 01:05:24.760
No significant effect on physical
performance or muscle strength.

01:05:24.760 --> 01:05:27.480
What does work?
Exercise.

01:05:27.480 --> 01:05:29.360
When it comes to
age-related muscle loss,

01:05:29.360 --> 01:05:32.480
it’s use it or lose it.

01:05:32.480 --> 01:05:35.880
But maybe exercise
plus protein?

01:05:35.880 --> 01:05:36.720
No.

01:05:36.720 --> 01:05:39.840
Protein supplementation
does not significantly augment

01:05:39.840 --> 01:05:42.440
the effects of strength
training in older adults.

01:05:42.440 --> 01:05:49.840
In fact, one of my Anti-Aging 8
is protein restriction.

01:05:49.840 --> 01:05:58.240
Why do longevity experts like Valter Longo
recommend people cut down on protein

01:05:58.240 --> 01:06:00.360
to live longer?

01:06:00.360 --> 01:06:02.760
Here’s what Luigi Fontana
wrote in his book.

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:06.320
He’s the director of another
leading longevity research center.

01:06:06.320 --> 01:06:09.800
Excess protein may
not mass your muscles,

01:06:09.800 --> 01:06:15.320
but it will accelerate aging.

01:06:15.320 --> 01:06:20.840
How do you boost the
pro-longevity hormone FGF21?

01:06:20.840 --> 01:06:24.080
Dietary protein restriction.

01:06:24.080 --> 01:06:28.840
How do you suppress the
age-accelerating enzyme IGF-1?

01:06:28.840 --> 01:06:31.880
You do it through dietary
protein restriction.

01:06:31.880 --> 01:06:35.400
How do you suppress the
so-called motor of aging, mTOR?

01:06:35.400 --> 01:06:38.880
Dietary protein restriction.

01:06:38.880 --> 01:06:42.200
In fact, protein restriction was
the only intervention I could find

01:06:42.200 --> 01:06:46.160
that could slow down all
11 of the aging pathways,

01:06:46.160 --> 01:06:49.960
which is good news because
it’s easier to cut back on protein

01:06:49.960 --> 01:06:51.040
than it is to cut
back on everything

01:06:51.040 --> 01:06:53.680
and walk around
starving all the time.

01:06:53.680 --> 01:06:58.040
And, it may even
be easier than that.

01:06:58.040 --> 01:07:03.840
Most or all of the life-extending
benefits of protein restriction

01:07:03.840 --> 01:07:07.920
may be due to just restricting
particular amino acids like methionine.

01:07:07.920 --> 01:07:12.320
In that case, you could even
keep your protein intake the same

01:07:12.320 --> 01:07:14.360
but just swap sources,

01:07:14.360 --> 01:07:22.520
from animal proteins like meat
to plant-based sources like beans.

01:07:22.520 --> 01:07:23.920
So, in humans,

01:07:24.760 --> 01:07:29.680
methionine restriction can be achieved
using a predominately plant-based diet,

01:07:29.680 --> 01:07:35.880
which makes methionine restriction
feasible as a life-extension strategy.

01:07:35.880 --> 01:07:39.400
Of course, drug companies
look at this same data and think:

01:07:39.400 --> 01:07:47.200
What pharmaceuticals can we develop
to mimic these dietary interventions?

01:07:47.200 --> 01:07:51.920
Anything else to maintain muscle mass
other than greens and machines?

01:07:51.920 --> 01:07:53.920
How about cocoa beans!

01:07:54.480 --> 01:07:57.400
Older men and women randomized
to a single tablespoon (22 g)

01:07:57.400 --> 01:07:59.480
of regular cocoa
powder a day

01:07:59.480 --> 01:08:02.080
experienced a significant
improvement in muscle strength,

01:08:02.080 --> 01:08:06.520
muscle mass, and all four
tests of physical performance.

01:08:06.520 --> 01:08:10.760
And no, the study was
not funded by Hershey’s.

01:08:10.760 --> 01:08:14.040
Unfortunately, the tastiest
cocoa doesn’t work as well.

01:08:14.040 --> 01:08:18.800
The control groups here were given
the same amount of highly Dutched cocoa—

01:08:18.800 --> 01:08:20.240
alkalized cocoa,

01:08:20.240 --> 01:08:23.160
where some of the bitter compounds
removed in the Dutching process

01:08:23.160 --> 01:08:27.040
are the very flavonoids
responsible for the benefits.

01:08:27.040 --> 01:08:29.840
But natural cocoa is so good

01:08:29.840 --> 01:08:33.640
it can improve walking performance
in those with peripheral artery disease,

01:08:33.640 --> 01:08:35.600
not only by
improving blood flow,

01:08:35.600 --> 01:08:37.360
but, as muscle
biopsies showed,

01:08:37.360 --> 01:08:40.160
by improving
mitochondrial activity.

01:08:40.160 --> 01:08:43.960
This is consistent with improved
mitochondrial structure

01:08:43.960 --> 01:08:46.880
as well in biopsies taken
from people’s quads.

01:08:46.880 --> 01:08:54.560
Though this study was
actually funded by Hershey’s.

01:08:54.560 --> 01:08:57.040
What else can cocoa do?

01:08:57.040 --> 01:09:00.080
Increase the circulation
within our skin,

01:09:00.080 --> 01:09:03.680
boosting it 70 percent
within two hours,

01:09:03.680 --> 01:09:05.320
after less than a tablespoon.

01:09:05.320 --> 01:09:08.000
Do that every day and
within three months,

01:09:08.000 --> 01:09:11.280
you end up with significantly
improved skin thickness,

01:09:11.280 --> 01:09:15.240
density, and hydration,
but no change in wrinkles.

01:09:15.240 --> 01:09:17.840
However, keep it
up for six months,

01:09:17.840 --> 01:09:20.480
and you do see an
improvement in wrinkles,

01:09:20.480 --> 01:09:22.920
a significant decrease
of wrinkle depth,

01:09:22.920 --> 01:09:26.800
along with an improvement in
skin elasticity within 12 weeks—

01:09:26.800 --> 01:09:30.280
all by making your life
a little more chocolatey,

01:09:30.280 --> 01:09:34.960
by just adding some natural
cocoa powder to your daily diet.

01:09:34.960 --> 01:09:39.320
I put it in my oatmeal with some
canned water-packed tart cherries

01:09:39.320 --> 01:09:43.440
for a chocolate-covered
cherry sensation.

01:09:43.440 --> 01:09:45.960
What else can we
do for our skin?

01:09:45.960 --> 01:09:48.160
Well, there certainly
are other antioxidant,

01:09:48.160 --> 01:09:50.920
anti-inflammatory,
glycotoxin-free foods;

01:09:50.920 --> 01:09:53.920
so, when patients
inquire about a diet

01:09:53.920 --> 01:09:56.960
that might contribute
to younger-looking skin,

01:09:56.960 --> 01:09:58.680
evidence supports
a recommendation

01:09:58.680 --> 01:10:01.160
to follow a whole food,
plant-based diet.

01:10:01.160 --> 01:10:08.080
But the most important thing to
prevent skin aging is avoid the sun.

01:10:08.080 --> 01:10:14.600
Exposure to sunlight may account
for 90 percent of visible skin aging.

01:10:14.600 --> 01:10:16.920
That’s why
dermatologists agree

01:10:16.920 --> 01:10:19.720
there’s nothing more important
to slow the signs of aging

01:10:19.720 --> 01:10:23.920
than to protect your
skin from the sun.

01:10:23.920 --> 01:10:25.160
Check out this trucker,

01:10:25.160 --> 01:10:27.760
who spent years getting more
sun on the left side of his face

01:10:27.760 --> 01:10:29.920
through the driver’s
side window.

01:10:29.920 --> 01:10:34.080
So, the gold standard is considered
to be the use of sunscreens

01:10:34.080 --> 01:10:37.720
in the daytime and
“retinoids” at night.

01:10:37.720 --> 01:10:42.520
They’re talking about a prescription-
only form of topical vitamin-A,

01:10:42.520 --> 01:10:44.640
also known as
all-trans-retinoic-acid,

01:10:44.640 --> 01:10:47.560
or tretinoin
(sold as Retin-A),

01:10:47.560 --> 01:10:53.440
that has been widely proven
to improve skin appearance.

01:10:53.440 --> 01:11:00.040
The only problem is
that it may kill you.

01:11:00.040 --> 01:11:02.440
“Topical Tretinoin Therapy
and All-Cause Mortality”,

01:11:02.440 --> 01:11:05.160
a randomized controlled trial
had to be stopped early,

01:11:05.160 --> 01:11:08.880
because significantly more people
were dying in the retinoic acid group,

01:11:08.880 --> 01:11:10.880
compared to the
placebo-cream group.

01:11:10.880 --> 01:11:12.040
When applied topically,

01:11:12.040 --> 01:11:14.960
1 to 8 percent is absorbed
into the bloodstream.

01:11:14.960 --> 01:11:16.600
Could it be
killing people?

01:11:16.600 --> 01:11:18.200
While the debate
continues

01:11:18.200 --> 01:11:21.320
as to whether the increase in deaths
was just some statistical fluke

01:11:21.320 --> 01:11:23.160
or a real biological effect,

01:11:23.160 --> 01:11:29.160
tretinoin continues to be banned
in Europe for cosmetic purposes.

01:11:29.160 --> 01:11:33.080
So, what can we use?

01:11:33.080 --> 01:11:37.360
Topical niacinamide,
also known as nicotinamide,

01:11:37.360 --> 01:11:42.480
is described as one of the best-studied
anti-aging skin cream ingredients

01:11:42.480 --> 01:11:45.200
(which is not
saying much),

01:11:45.200 --> 01:11:48.480
but it has been shown
to reduce yellowing,

01:11:48.480 --> 01:11:52.920
wrinkling, blotchiness,
and dark spots in aging facial skin,

01:11:52.920 --> 01:11:57.840
for example, significantly reducing
crow’s feet wrinkles around the eyes.

01:11:57.840 --> 01:12:00.040
This was a
split-face study

01:12:00.040 --> 01:12:04.200
in which 64 percent of the
niacinamide-side eye wrinkles

01:12:04.200 --> 01:12:06.160
had moderate or
marked improvement,

01:12:06.160 --> 01:12:11.120
compared to zero percent
of the placebo-side eyes.

01:12:11.120 --> 01:12:16.640
Topical vitamin C also offers similar
improvements over placebo in wrinkles,

01:12:16.640 --> 01:12:19.600
sallowness, and
skin tone or firmness.

01:12:19.600 --> 01:12:22.480
Unfortunately, vitamin C
is unstable in creams,

01:12:22.480 --> 01:12:25.200
turning an unsightly
brown when it oxidizes,

01:12:25.200 --> 01:12:26.200
limiting its shelf life.

01:12:26.200 --> 01:12:28.360
So instead, what the
skin care industry does,

01:12:28.360 --> 01:12:29.800
the skin care industry,

01:12:29.800 --> 01:12:33.760
they use the stable
vitamin C esters or derivatives,

01:12:33.760 --> 01:12:37.600
but there’s no evidence that these
compounds have comparable effects,

01:12:37.600 --> 01:12:39.640
likely because they’re
poorly absorbed

01:12:40.240 --> 01:12:42.600
and only minimally convert
to the active form.

01:12:42.600 --> 01:12:45.080
But the good news is you
can make your own.

01:12:45.080 --> 01:12:51.880
The 10 percent solution used in the study
retails for a ridiculous $127 an ounce (30 ml),

01:12:51.880 --> 01:12:57.080
but you can make a DIY solution
more than two thousand times cheaper

01:12:57.080 --> 01:13:00.120
by simply buying
ascorbic acid in bulk

01:13:00.120 --> 01:13:02.680
and mixing three grams
into 30 grams of water,

01:13:02.680 --> 01:13:06.280
at a cost of about
a nickel per ounce (30 ml).

01:13:06.280 --> 01:13:08.320
Drip a few drops into
the palm of your hand

01:13:08.320 --> 01:13:10.880
and use your fingertips to
apply it over your face,

01:13:10.880 --> 01:13:12.000
neck, and upper chest daily.

01:13:12.000 --> 01:13:13.760
(Just don’t get
it in your eyes.)

01:13:13.760 --> 01:13:16.560
And look, you can do the
same thing with niacinamide,

01:13:16.560 --> 01:13:20.240
which can be even
more ridiculously priced.

01:13:20.240 --> 01:13:22.320
But you can buy
it in bulk as well,

01:13:22.320 --> 01:13:26.560
at a 5,000 percent discount.

01:13:26.560 --> 01:13:28.840
I can go on and on,
and the book certainly does,

01:13:28.840 --> 01:13:34.760
but let me close out by
going back to the basics.

01:13:34.760 --> 01:13:38.320
From the anti-aging journal
Rejuvenation Research:

01:13:38.320 --> 01:13:42.800
“Finally, a regimen to extend
human life expectancy.”

01:13:42.800 --> 01:13:46.880
Was it some exotic new stem
cell treatment or gene therapy?

01:13:46.880 --> 01:13:50.880
No, it was a reference
to this Harvard analysis.

01:13:50.880 --> 01:13:53.560
More than a hundred thousand men
and women were followed for decades,

01:13:53.560 --> 01:13:56.000
and even just a few
basic lifestyle behaviors

01:13:56.000 --> 01:14:03.200
appeared to translate into 12 to 14
extra years for the average 50-year-old.

01:14:03.200 --> 01:14:04.480
Even at age 50,

01:14:04.480 --> 01:14:08.240
you may be able to add an
extra dozen years to your lifespan.

01:14:08.240 --> 01:14:10.600
Even at age 70,

01:14:10.600 --> 01:14:14.200
there could still be about
ten extra years on the table.

01:14:14.200 --> 01:14:17.440
And if you clean up
your life before 50,

01:14:17.440 --> 01:14:20.240
nearly 18 years may
be up-for-grabs,

01:14:20.240 --> 01:14:22.640
based on simple
common-sense behaviors.

01:14:22.640 --> 01:14:24.560
That’s the kind
of life extension

01:14:24.560 --> 01:14:28.280
extrapolating from some
of these lab animal experiments,

01:14:28.280 --> 01:14:32.680
but after decades of research and
hundreds of millions of dollars later,

01:14:32.680 --> 01:14:37.680
efforts to translate those results
into humans have largely been in vain.

01:14:37.680 --> 01:14:41.000
Yet, here we are
with human data

01:14:41.000 --> 01:14:44.760
suggesting dramatic life
extension is available to all of us.

01:14:44.760 --> 01:14:46.800
Right here, right now.

01:14:46.800 --> 01:14:53.960
The trillion-dollar pill that anti-aging
biotech has been promising us is here,

01:14:53.960 --> 01:15:00.680
it just effectively has to be administered
in the produce aisle and the gym.

01:15:00.680 --> 01:15:05.480
Turning back the clock,
not with a drug or a DeLorean,

01:15:05.480 --> 01:15:08.720
but just by eating and
living more healthfully.

01:15:08.720 --> 01:15:14.000
A midlife shift between
the ages of 45 and 64

01:15:14.000 --> 01:15:18.520
to even the barest
of minimums,

01:15:18.520 --> 01:15:20.840
the barest of minimums.

01:15:20.840 --> 01:15:21.720
So, what are
we talking about?

01:15:21.720 --> 01:15:23.520
At least 5 servings of
fruits and vegetables a day,

01:15:23.520 --> 01:15:25.600
walking even just
20 minutes a day,

01:15:25.600 --> 01:15:26.680
maintaining a
healthy weight,

01:15:26.680 --> 01:15:27.560
and not smoking,

01:15:27.560 --> 01:15:30.640
can result in a substantial
reduction in mortality

01:15:30.640 --> 01:15:33.120
even in the immediate future.

01:15:33.120 --> 01:15:35.360
We’re talking a 40
percent lower risk of dying

01:15:35.360 --> 01:15:37.440
in the subsequent
four years.

01:15:37.440 --> 01:15:40.920
The researchers conclude that
making the necessary changes

01:15:40.920 --> 01:15:45.360
to adhere to a healthy lifestyle
is extremely worthwhile,

01:15:45.360 --> 01:15:48.960
and middle-age is
certainly not too late to act.

01:15:48.960 --> 01:15:52.960
You hold the power.

01:15:52.960 --> 01:15:56.680
OK. So, I was only able to
get to about 100 studies.

01:15:56.680 --> 01:15:59.600
Just 12,900 to go!

01:15:59.600 --> 01:16:04.880
For the other 99 percent,
you’ll have to wait for the book.

01:16:04.880 --> 01:16:07.680
But it’s available
for preorder now,

01:16:07.680 --> 01:16:10.960
and all proceeds I receive
from the sales of all my books

01:16:10.960 --> 01:16:13.640
are donated directly to charity.

01:16:13.640 --> 01:16:22.200
I just want you and your loved
ones to enjoy the longest, healthiest life.

